# tools/ales_trim_silence_witness.rish — the silence stripper: segment the clip through ALES95, then cut every silent run (ALES96).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES96.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260814/20260814-232010_fill-ales96-lotus-silence-stripper.md
# Stands on: lotus/segment.rye (ALES95 — the voice-activity segmenter), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 — the Clip and cut), lotus/voiced.rye (ALES94 — the .silent
# verdict), the band read through lotus/schmitt.rye's precheck (ALES92). ALES95 read a whole clip into a run-length sequence of regions yet changed not one sample;
# its own scope note named the first tool built on it — a silence trimmer that drops the .silent runs. This is the FIRST Lotus rung to turn an analysis result back
# into an edit. (Named trim_silence to stand clear of ALES9's strip.rye, the channel strip's mute/solo.)
#
# lotus/trim_silence.rye segments [0, clip.len) into frames of frame_len samples through ALES95, then walks the runs BACK TO FRONT and cuts every .silent run
# through ALES2's cut (back-to-front keeps every earlier run's index valid, since a cut shifts only samples after it). The crux is that it invents no new
# measurement and no new sample — the trimmed clip equals a hand-built concatenation of the non-silent runs read from the original, so it can never drift from the
# segmenter it composes. The selftest proves it: a mixed clip loses exactly its silent samples and keeps the rest contiguous (the interior gap closed) across many
# frame lengths, floors, and splits (the crux); an all-silent clip trims to empty; a no-silent clip is unchanged; the length law holds (trimmed length equals
# original minus the silent samples); an empty clip, a ragged frame length, an inverted band, and an illegal floor each refuse by name, the clip untouched.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_trim_silence_witness.rish

say "ales-trim-silence: Language EN — ALES95 read a whole clip into a run-length sequence of silent, voiced, and unvoiced regions yet changed not one sample; its own scope note named the first tool built on it — a silence trimmer that drops the .silent runs. That is the most common single gesture in all of audio editing: trim silence, the dead-air remover every recorder and podcast tool ships. This is the FIRST Lotus rung to turn an ANALYSIS result back into an EDIT."
say "ales-trim-silence: Style Radiant — the rule is stated positively: segment [0, clip.len) into frames, then walk the runs BACK TO FRONT and cut every .silent run (back-to-front keeps every earlier run's index valid, since a cut shifts only samples after it). The crux stands out: it invents no new measurement and no new sample — the trimmed clip equals a hand-built concatenation of the non-silent runs read from the original, so it can never drift from the segmenter it composes."
say "ales-trim-silence: Lens TAME — one mono clip of i16 PCM segmented through ALES95 (all its validation — precheck for band/range, the floor ceiling, the dividing frame length, the empty-clip BadFrame — runs before any cut), then edited only by removing whole classified-silent frames through ALES2's own cut; no sample fabricated, nothing reordered; the error vocabulary is the segmenter's SegmentError and the editor's EditError reused by name; siloed to lotus/, not a transcript or a real adaptive VAD, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/trim_silence"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/trim_silence.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-trim-silence: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/trim_silence.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-trim-silence: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-trim-silence: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-trim-silence' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-trim-silence: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-trim-silence line"
